# Scene
trueqbit with Claude (Opus 5.5), 2026-11-19, after the review of #1524
was posted: trueqbit asked whether when_all() could be a polyfill for
C++26's std::execution::when_all.

# Discussed
- Rejected as a polyfill: [exec.when.all] (eel.is C++ draft) takes a pack
  of senders, no range; completes with the children's values
  concatenated; the first error requests stop of the others through a
  stop source. Ours (functional/coroutines/when_all.h:77) takes a
  std::vector<task<T>>, returns task<std::vector<T>>, lets every task
  finish and rethrows the first exception, without cancellation. Keeping
  the standard's contract would make task a sender, a redesign of the
  runtime; the name alone would promise semantics ours lacks. It argues
  for (f): async_when_all() reads as a different thing, not a stand-in.

# Later
- Interoperation of the asynchronous task with C++26 std::execution, such
  as task awaitable inside a sender pipeline: an issue of its own, after
  #1524, alongside the existing runtime rather than replacing it.

